dsmr-qmail-run (1.0-13) trixie; urgency=medium

  * newaliases: rebuild /etc/aliases.cdb instead of doing nothing. The previous
    no-op stub left the alias database stale, which became a hazard once
    dsmr-localfirst began rejecting unknown recipients at RCPT time by checking
    the fastforward cdb: an alias added to /etc/aliases but never compiled into
    /etc/aliases.cdb would be rejected at SMTP time. newaliases now delegates
    to fastforward's qmail-newaliases (when installed) to recompile the
    world-readable cdb from /etc/aliases, and stays a no-op only when
    fastforward is absent or there is no /etc/aliases.
  * Recommend fastforward so the alias compiler is present by default on stack
    hosts that route system aliases through the fastforward catch-all.

 -- Brian O'Reilly <fade@deepsky.com>  Fri, 05 Jun 2026 02:06:42 +0000

dsmr-qmail-run (1.0-12) trixie; urgency=high

  * Make system-first recipient validation opt-in.  The smtpd and submission run
    scripts now switch to localfirst-rcptcheck only when control/localfirst has
    "1" on its first line; the mere presence of the binary no longer changes the
    recipient gate.  Installing or upgrading the stack on a host that has not
    opted in therefore keeps chkuser exactly as before — an upgrade must never
    silently change which recipients are accepted.  To enable system-first
    routing on a host, write "1" to /var/qmail/control/localfirst and cycle the
    smtpd and submission services.

 -- Brian O'Reilly <fade@deepsky.com>  Thu, 04 Jun 2026 17:45:00 +0000

